{"cuisine/antigua-and-barbuda-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/antigua-and-barbuda-cuisine","n":"Antigua and Barbuda cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Antigua and Barb.","intro":[["Antigua and Barbuda cuisine refers to the cuisines of the Caribbean islands Antigua and Barbuda. The national dish is fungee and pepperpot. Fungee is a dish similar to Italian polenta, made mostly with cornmeal. Other local dishes include ducana, seasoned rice, saltfish and lobster (from Barbuda). There are also local confectioneries which include sugar cake, fudge, raspberry and tamarind stew, and peanut brittle."],["These foods are indigenous to Antigua and Barbuda and to some other Caribbean countries, the local diet has diversified and includes local dishes of Jamaica, such as jerk meats, or Trinidad, such as roti, and specialties of other Caribbean countries."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"antigua-and-barbuda-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antigua_and_Barbuda_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas"},"cuisine/bhojpuri-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/bhojpuri-cuisine","n":"Bhojpuri cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["North Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-indian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["Bhojpuri cuisine is a style of food preparation common among the Bhojpuri people of Bihar, Jharkhand and eastern Uttar Pradesh in India, and also the Terai region of Nepal. Bhojpuri foods are mostly mild and tend to be less hot in terms of spices used. The cuisine consists of both vegetable and non-vegetarian dishes."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"bhojpuri-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bhojpuri_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["North Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-indian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Litti (dish)","dish/litti-dish"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Atta","ingredient/atta"],["Besan","ingredient/besan"],["Brinjal","ingredient/brinjal"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Cilantro","ingredient/cilantro"],["Ghee","ingredient/ghee"],["Gram flour","ingredient/gram-flour"],["Mustard oil","ingredient/mustard-oil"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Sattu","ingredient/sattu"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["North Indian cuisine","cuisine/north-indian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/cuisine-of-basilicata":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-basilicata","n":"Basilicatan (Lucanian) cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Italy","intro":[["The cuisine of Basilicata, or Lucanian cuisine, is the cuisine of the Basilicata region of Italy. It is mainly based on the use of pork and sheep meat, legumes, cereals and vegetables, with the addition of aromas such as hot peppers, powdered raw peppers and horseradish. The local gastronomy is, for historical-cultural reasons, typically peasant, based on simple recipes and on the culture of reuse, in particular of meat and bread."],["Some dishes have undergone variations and enrichments in modern times, losing the connotations of \"poor\" cooking which characterized them in the past. The most ancient manuscript available about Lucanian cooking dates back to 1524, by Antonio Camuria from Lagonegro, cook at the service of the Carafa family."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-basilicata"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_Basilicata"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Inside the Rustic Kitchen: Basilicata recipes","ref":"site/insidetherustickitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"insidetherustickitchen.com","note":"Italian home-cooking blog's dedicated section of dishes from Basilicata.","url":"https://www.insidetherustickitchen.com/category/basilicata-recipes","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Tumact me tulez","dish/tumact-me-tulez"],["U' pastizz 'rtunnar","dish/u-pastizz-rtunnar"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Anchovies as food","ingredient/anchovies-as-food"],["Breadcrumbs","ingredient/breadcrumbs"],["Pasta","ingredient/pasta"],["Tagliatelle","ingredient/tagliatelle"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Walnut","ingredient/walnut"],["Cheese","ingredient/cheese"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine"},"cuisine/maltese-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/maltese-cuisine","n":"Maltese cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Malta","intro":[["Maltese cuisine reflects Maltese history, showing strong Italian influences, as well as influences from Spanish, French, Provençal and other Mediterranean cuisines, with some later British culinary influence. Being positioned along important trade routes, having to import most of its foodstuffs, and having to cater to the resident foreign powers who ruled the islands opened Maltese cuisine to outside influences. The traditional Maltese stewed rabbit, stuffat tal-fenek, is often identified as the national dish."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"maltese-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maltese_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Malta: Mediterranean Recipes from the Islands","ref":"cookbook/malta-mediterranean-recipes-from-the-islands","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Simon Bajada, 2019","note":"Recipes and food 